ume payload flood (e.g., HTTP\u2011GET flood or reflective attack). <br>\u2022 Flow duration\u202f\u2248\u202f1.85\u202fs with long inter\u2011arrival times (Bwd IAT\u202f\u2248\u202f926\u202fk\u202f\u00b5s) indicating bursty traffic. |\n\nThe convergence of **near\u2011perfect ensemble probability**, **extreme model agreement**, **high anomaly & isolation scores**, and **abnormal packet\u2011size distribution** makes this flow a **high\u2011confidence DDoS indicator** despite its modest packet count.\n\n**3. Correlated activity**  \n- All related events share:  \n  - **Destination port\u202f80** (HTTP)  \n  - **Critical severity, risk\u202f=\u202f99**  \n  - Ensemble probabilities \u2265\u202f0.9998 (many at 1.0)  \n  - Labels alternating between **DDoS** and **DoS\u202fHulk** \u2013 both volumetric attacks on the same service.  \n- Temporal correlation is implied by the single evidence packet (all events appear in the same analysis window).  \n- The pattern suggests a coordinated flood against the web service, possibly a multi\u2011vector attack (classic SYN/ACK flood mixed with HTTP\u2011GET \u201cHulk\u201d bursts).\n\n**4. Analyst\u2011reviewable next steps**  \n\n1. **Source\u2011IP & Geo\u2011lookup** \u2013 Pull the raw NetFlow / packet\u2011capture records for the listed event IDs and map source IPs. Identify any single\u2011source dominance or geographically dispersed bot\u2011net signatures.  \n2. **Firewall / IDS log cross\u2011check** \u2013 Query the perimeter firewall and IDS for alerts on port\u202f80 during the flow\u2011duration window. Verify whether rate\u2011limiting or existing DDoS mitigation rules were triggered or missed.  \n3. **Packet\u2011level inspection & signature creation** \u2013 Extract the full payload of the high\u2011size backward packets (\u2248\u202f11\u202fKB) for protocol analysis (e.g., malformed HTTP headers, large GET/POST bodies). Use findings to draft a temporary ACL or rate\u2011limit rule that can be reviewed and applied by the network team if the traffic is confirmed malicious.  \n\n*All actions should be performed under analyst supervision; no automatic blocking is enacted by the system.*",
